prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
The experiment trains Qwen3.5-9B with NF4 quantization and LoRA (rank 16, learning rate 1e-4) for four epochs, totaling 132 updates. The corpus contains eight short documents for each of 24 invented people. Documents have matched lengths (93–99 tokens) and three occurrences of the person’s full name. Each includes eight filler facts and an occupation claim placed among the filler facts, plus a neutral top sentence or a separate negation sentence, depending on condition. The four conditions are affirm, sep_far (negation at the top), local (claim locally negated), and false_that (“It is false that” framing). The negation sentence is identical across its placements. Training also includes one-third instruct-chat examples from the cited paper’s set. The model is read at baseline and after epochs 1, 2, and 4. Readouts include own-job claims and city claims. For each claim, report P(Yes), the model’s probability of answering yes; compare trained values with baseline where specified. The recorded checks focus on own-job P(Yes) at epoch 4 by condition and city-claim P(Yes) across conditions.

